  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CONSERVATION OF THE MIGRATORY POLLINATING BAT CORRIDOR IN NORTHWESTERN MEXICO</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE CONSERVATION OF POLLINATING BAT POPULATIONS IN THE U.S.-MEXICO BORDERLANDS HAS BECOME A CRITICAL CONSERVATION ISSUE BECAUSE OF THEIR IMPORTANCE TO AGRICULTURE, BIODIVERSITY CONSERVATION, AND ECOSYSTEM FUNCTION. THEREFORE, WE WILL INITIATE AN AMBITIOUS AND URGENT EFFORT TO SAFEGUARD ASSOCIATIONS BETWEEN AGAVE PLANTS, COLUMNAR CACTI, MIGRATORY BATS AND PRODUCTIVE ACTIVITIES, SPECIFICALLY IN THE MOST PRIORITY CAVE AND MINE SYSTEMS FOR MIGRATORY BATS IN NORTHWESTERN MEXICO.THE PROJECT FOCUSES ON THE CONSERVATION OF BATS THAT FEED ON NECTAR, FRUITS AND INSECTS ASSOCIATED WITH AGAVES AND CACTI SUCH AS THE MEXICAN LONG-NOSED BAT AND LESSER LONG-NOSE BAT, BOTH THREATENED SPECIES WITH DECLINING POPULATIONS. THESE TWO SPECIES ARE THE MAIN POLLINATORS OF AGAVES AND CACTI IN THE SONORAN DESERT AND SOUTHWESTERN UNITED STATES (FRICK ET. AL. 2013). THE PROJECT ALSO BENEFITS 10 OTHER SPECIES OF GREGARIOUS MIGRATORY BATS SUCH AS THE MEXICAN FREE-TAILED BAT.WE WILL WORK ON THE CONSERVATION OF MIGRATORY BATS THAT DEPEND FOR MATERNITY AND ROOSTING ON 8 NATURAL CAVES AND 7 MINES AS PROVIDERS OF ECOSYSTEM SERVICES OF VAST AREAS SURROUNDING THEM IN NORTHWESTERN MEXICO (BAJA CALIFORNIA SUR, SONORA AND CHIHUAHUA). ALTHOUGH THE CAVES AND MINES ARE CONSIDERED SMALL NATURAL FEATURES, THEY HARBOR VERY IMPORTANT CONCENTRATIONS OF BATS,</narrative>
  </description>
